# Dependencies and env
backend/.env
backend/__pycache__
backend/**/__pycache__
landing/
.git
.gitignore
*.md
# ...except the agreement, which the app READS at runtime rather than being
# documentation about it. Last match wins in this file, so this line has to
# stay below the *.md above.
!docs/legal/*.md
.env
.env.*

# Real data and real credentials belonging to whoever ran the build. `COPY
# backend/ backend/` copies the WORKING TREE, not the git index, so every one of
# these ships inside the image even though .gitignore keeps them out of the repo:
# a 4 MB team.db carrying the builder's live onboarding funnel, the staff
# password store, the demo patient roster. None of them is needed to boot.
#
# Verified not needed at runtime, one by one:
#   *.db          team_store.connect_team_db and community.store.CommunityStore
#                 both mkdir the parent and sqlite3.connect(), which CREATES an
#                 empty file, then run their schema migrations. In production
#                 TEAM_DB_PATH / COMMUNITY_DB_PATH point at the volume, so a
#                 baked-in copy is never even opened; it is only stale data
#                 sitting where a misconfigured deploy can find it. Nothing
#                 tracked in git matches (`git ls-files | grep '\.db$'` is empty),
#                 so no seed corpus or fixture is caught by this.
#   auth_users.json        auth.py:184 / routers/admin.py:98 both read it behind
#                          USERS_FILE.exists() and return {} when absent.
#   demo_patient_store.json main.py:_load_demo_patient_store_snapshot returns
#                          early on `not os.path.isfile(path)`; the roster is
#                          re-seeded at startup regardless.
#   _assetstore/           a stray local blob dir; production points
#                          ASCLEPIUS_ASSET_STORE at the mounted volume.
**/*.db
**/*.db-journal
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm
backend/auth_users.json
backend/demo_patient_store.json
backend/asclepius/_assetstore/

# The two dotenv rules above are ROOT-anchored (a .dockerignore pattern does not
# cross `/` unless it says `**`), so backend/.env needed its own line and
# anything deeper had none at all. Catch every dotenv wherever it sits, including
# the .bak copies that are the ones most likely to hold real keys.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/sendgrid.env

# Virtualenvs are hundreds of MB of the builder's machine, and pip/keyring state
# under them can carry credentials. The image installs deps from requirements.txt
# in its own cached layer.
**/.venv/
**/venv/
**/.DS_Store

# Last match wins, and the `**/.env.*` above would otherwise swallow the one
# dotenv file that is safe to ship (it holds no values, only documentation).
!.env.example
